`
			},
			checks: [
				{ id: 'plan-exists', kind: 'exists', selector: '#wp-plan' },
				{ id: 'no-blanks', kind: 'text', selector: '#wp-plan', matches: '^[^…]*$' },
				{ id: 'tagline', kind: 'text', selector: '#site-description', matches: '\\S{3,}' },
				{ id: 'menu-sections', kind: 'field', selector: '#menu a', field: 'sections' },
				{ id: 'action-button', kind: 'field', selector: '#content a.button', field: 'mainAction.label' },
				{ id: 'sections-planned', kind: 'count', selector: '#map tr.sec', min: { brief: 'sections' } },
				{ id: 'kind-named', kind: 'text', selector: '#map td.kind', matches: '[Pp]age|[Cc]ategor' },
				{ id: 'in-menu', kind: 'text', selector: '#map td.menu', matches: '^\\s*(?:[Yy]es|[Nn]o)' },
				{ id: 'plugins-count', kind: 'count', selector: '#plugins li.plugin', min: 2, max: 5 },
				{ id: 'plugins-reasons', kind: 'text', selector: '#plugins li.plugin .why', matches: '\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+' },
				{ id: 'action-plugin', kind: 'text', selector: '#action-by', matches: '[Pp]lugin|[Bb]lock|[Ff]orm|[Bb]utton|[Ww]idget' },
				{ id: 'action-where', kind: 'text', selector: '#action-where', matches: '[Hh]ome|[Pp]age|[Ss]idebar|[Ff]ooter|[Mm]enu|[Hh]eader' },
				{ id: 'tone-note', kind: 'text', selector: '#tone-note', matches: '\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+' }
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'How does a page differ from a post in WordPress?',
				options: [
					'A post is tied to time and joins a feed (news, an article), while a page is a separate permanent section (“About”, “Contact”)',
					'A page is stored in a file and a post in the database',
					'A post cannot be edited'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Posts run as a feed by date and go into categories and tags, while pages stand alone and form a hierarchy.',
					'No: both are stored in the database.',
					'No: both are edited in the same way.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each area of a page to the theme file that draws it',
				pairs: [
					['Header and menu', 'header.php'],
					['The loop of posts on the home page', 'index.php'],
					['The sidebar with widgets', 'sidebar.php'],
					['The footer', 'footer.php']
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'You need to add an enquiry form. Is that the theme’s job or a plugin’s?',
				options: [
					'A plugin’s: a form is a capability, not design, and it should survive a change of theme',
					'The theme’s: the form has to be built into header.php',
					'The WordPress core’s: it already has a form'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. A theme is responsible for looks and a plugin for capabilities. If the form is built into the theme, it disappears when the theme changes.',
					'No: then the form is tied to the theme and will not survive replacing it.',
					'No: the core has no enquiry form, but there are plugins for that.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'How do plugins fit into the way WordPress works?',
				options: [
					'Through hooks: at the right places the core and the theme say “something can be added or changed here”, and a plugin attaches',
					'Plugins replace files of the core',
					'Plugins run on the editor’s computer'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. There are two kinds of hook: “actions” (do something at this point) and “filters” (change this value before it is shown).',
					'No: editing the core would break at the very next update.',
					'No: plugins are server code.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'Why did WordPress become the most widespread CMS?',
				options: [
					'It runs on the cheapest hosting with PHP and MySQL',
					'It has a huge choice of themes and plugins',
					'It is open source and free, and many people know how to use it',
					'It is better protected against hacking than any other system'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 2],
				why: [
					'Yes: a simple install on almost any host was its trump card from the start.',
					'Yes: capabilities are added with ready-made plugins instead of being programmed.',
					'Yes: an open license and a big community make help easy to find.',
					'No: because it is so widespread and has so many plugins, WordPress is attacked more often than most (the next lesson is about that).'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'How many plugins should a small site have?',
				options: [
					'As few as possible: each is someone else’s code that has to be updated, and it can slow the site down or break it',
					'As many as possible: then the site can do more',
					'Exactly twenty'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Keep a short list (a form, SEO, a cache, backups) and write a reason beside each.',
					'No: each plugin adds to the work of updating and to the surface for an attack.',
					'No: there is no right number as high as twenty.'
				]
			}
		],
		cards: [
			{
				id: 'cms-wp-origin',
				front: 'Where did WordPress come from, and what is it?',
				back: 'It appeared in 2003 as blogging software (Matt Mullenweg and Mike Little, continuing b2/cafelog). An open-source CMS in PHP and MySQL. Version 3.0 (June 2010) added custom post types and menus, and it grew from a blog tool into a CMS.'
			},
			{
				id: 'cms-wp-post-page',
				front: 'A post and a page in WordPress',
				back: 'A post runs in a feed by date and sits in categories and tags (news, articles). A page is a separate permanent section (“About”, “Contact”).'
			},
			{
				id: 'cms-wp-theme',
				front: 'What does a theme decide, and what is it made of?',
				back: 'A theme decides how the site looks. It is a set of PHP templates (header.php, index.php, sidebar.php, footer.php, single.php, page.php) and style.css. The loop of posts puts the data into them.'
			},
			{
				id: 'cms-wp-plugin',
				front: 'What does a plugin do, and how does it fit in?',
				back: 'It adds capabilities (a form, SEO, a cache, backups) by attaching to hooks: actions and filters. A form or SEO is written as a plugin so it survives a change of theme.'
			},
			{
				id: 'cms-wp-rule',
				front: 'What is the rule for plugins?',
				back: 'As few as possible, each with a written reason. Every plugin is someone else’s code with updates, and it affects speed and security.'
			}
		],
		sources: [
			{ title: 'WordPress Developer Resources: Theme Handbook, Getting Started', url: 'https://developer.wordpress.org/themes/getting-started/', licence: 'proprietary-link', kind: 'docs', lang: 'en' },
			{ title: 'WordPress: Template Hierarchy', url: 'https://developer.wordpress.org/themes/basics/template-hierarchy/', licence: 'proprietary-link', kind: 'docs', lang: 'en' },
			{ title: 'WordPress Plugin Handbook: Hooks', url: 'https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/hooks/', licence: 'proprietary-link', kind: 'docs', lang: 'en' },
			{ title: 'WordPress.org: History', url: 'https://wordpress.org/about/history/', licence: 'proprietary-link', kind: 'article', lang: 'en' },
			{ title: 'HTTP Archive Web Almanac 2024: CMS', url: 'https://almanac.httparchive.org/en/2024/cms', licence: 'CC-BY-SA', kind: 'article', lang: 'en' }
		],
